\section{Keeping the two languages in step}\label{sec:didactic-sync}

A bilingual source (\cref{sec:bilingual}) has one danger that two separate
files don't have in the same way, and that's a quiet one.
Somebody fixes a paragraph in one language, the document builds, both editions
look fine, and from then on they say different things.
Nothing in \LaTeX\ can notice that.
Neither can a script, in the sense that matters: no program can tell whether
two paragraphs say the same thing.
What a program can tell is whether one of them has \emph{changed} since
somebody last judged that they did.

So that's what we record.
On the line of each pair's first [[\begin]] stands a comment that only this
script writes,
\begin{minted}{text}
\begin{en} % sync en=3f9c1a sv=b20e7d
The cat sat.
\end{en}
\begin{sv}
Katten satt.
\end{sv}
\end{minted}
holding a short hash of each side as it was when the two last said the same
thing.
After a change to the English side, the script reports
\begin{minted}{text}
contents.nw:1: sv is behind
  en since the stamp (4f1c2ab): The [-cat-]{+dog+} sat.
  sv now: Katten satt.
\end{minted}
and it goes on saying so, in every build and from every clone, until somebody
has read the two and stamps the pair again.
No note has to survive between commits, or between people, since the state is
in the file.

\Cref{tab:didactic-sync-states} gives the whole of what can be reported.
The last row is a stamp that has lost its pair, see \cref{sec:sync-pairs}.

\begin{table}
  \centering
  \caption{%
    What the script reports for a pair, by whether each side still has the
    hash in its stamp.
    Only the first row leaves the exit status at zero.
  }\label{tab:didactic-sync-states}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \toprule
    \textbf{en} & \textbf{sv} & \textbf{Report} \\
    \midrule
    unchanged & unchanged & in sync \\
    changed & unchanged & sv is behind \\
    unchanged & changed & en is behind \\
    changed & changed & read both and stamp \\
    \multicolumn{2}{l}{no stamp} & new pair, stamp once both are written \\
    \multicolumn{2}{l}{a stamp, but no pair} & a stamp with no pair here \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The word \enquote{changed} carries a limit that the row \enquote{read both and
stamp} makes visible: when both sides have changed, the script knows only that
both have moved, not that they moved to the same place.
That's why a stamp is a judgement by whoever gives it, and why the script has
no way of stamping everything at once.
A command that stamps a whole file makes the report go away without anybody
having read anything, which is the very failure that the script exists to
prevent.

\subsection{Commands}

The script has three modes, and each of the following sections builds one of
them from the same reading of the source.
\begin{description}
  \item[State check] [[didactic-sync.py contents.nw ...]] reports on each pair
  (\cref{sec:sync-state}).
  The exit status is 0 if all pairs are in sync, 1 if any is not, and 2 if a
  file can't be read as blocks at all, so a book build can fail on it.
  With [[--list]] it only lists, and it exits with 0 even if something is
  behind, which is what a deck's build wants while the text is being worked on.
  With [[-v]] it also lists the pairs that are in sync.
  \item[Accepting] [[didactic-sync.py --stamp contents.nw:12]] stamps the one
  pair at that line (\cref{sec:sync-stamp}), after a translation, or to accept
  a
  fix of a typo in one language as needing no translation.
  \item[Branch check] [[didactic-sync.py origin/master...HEAD]] lists the pairs
  whose stamp a branch has refreshed while only one side changed
    (\cref{sec:sync-branch}): those acceptances, for the description of a pull
    request, so that somebody can look at them.
\end{description}

Builds find the script with [[kpsewhich didactic-sync.py]], so it's installed
next to [[didactic.sty]].

\subsection{The outline of the program}

Everything goes from text to pairs: the three modes differ only in what they
do with the pairs of a file (or of two versions of it).
A pair therefore has to be recognised in exactly one way, which is the reason
for reading git's old versions of a file with the same functions as the file
on disk.
\begin{description}
  \item[Reading] finds what \LaTeX\ will treat as blocks and as [[\ensv]], and
  ignores what it won't (\cref{sec:sync-reading,sec:sync-tr}).
  \item[Pairing] turns those into pairs with their stamps
  (\cref{sec:sync-pairs}).
  \item[The modes] are the state check with its word diff, stamping and the
  branch check
  (\cref{sec:sync-state,sec:sync-diff,sec:sync-stamp,sec:sync-branch}).
  \item[The command line] ties them together (\cref{sec:sync-cli}).
\end{description}

