\subsection{Reading a source: what is a block}\label{sec:sync-reading}

The languages are English and Swedish, and the names that the script looks
for, the environments and the short command, all come from one table, which
gives each command the codes of its two languages.
The codes are also the keys of a stamp, [[en=...]] and [[sv=...]].
A second pair, such as English and German, would start here, with
[[\ende]] for [[("en", "de")]]; what else it takes is in
dbosk/didactic\#105.
In this script, the [[Stamp]] and the [[Pair]] and the reports still know their
two languages by name, [[en]] and [[sv]]; in the package, the environments,
[[\didacticlanguage]] and the short command are made for those two
(\cref{sec:impl-bilingual}).

<<imports>>=
import re
<<constants>>=
PAIRS = {"ensv": ("en", "sv"), "didactictr": ("en", "sv")}
LANGUAGES = tuple(
    dict.fromkeys(code for pair in PAIRS.values() for code in pair)
)
LANGUAGE = "(" + "|".join(LANGUAGES) + ")"
@

The test of whether a line opens a block is what \LaTeX\ will do with it, and
that's less obvious in a literate source than it looks.
The deck on \LaTeX\ will show [[\begin{en}]] in a code chunk, and a chunk is
shown by [[minted]], which never sees it as a command, so it isn't a block;
the same goes for a [[minted]] environment in the prose, and for a line that's
commented out.
A pair that the script invents in such a place would ask for a stamp that
makes no sense, and a stamp written into a chunk would change the program.

That means a pass over the lines with a little state: are we in a code chunk,
or a verbatim environment, or a frame?
A chunk starts on a line of its own, [[@<<name>>=]], and it runs until a line
that starts with [[@]]; another chunk's start doesn't end the chunk mode, as
it's just a chunk in turn.
[[@@]] at the start of a line is noweb's escape for a literal [[@]] in the
code, so the [[@]] has to stand alone or be followed by a space.

<<constants>>=
CHUNK_START = re.compile(r"@<<.*>>=\s*$")
CHUNK_END = re.compile(r"@(\s|$)")
BLOCK_BEGIN = re.compile(r"\s*\\begin\{" + LANGUAGE + r"\}")
VERBATIM_BEGIN = re.compile(
    r"\s*\\begin\{(minted|lstlisting|comment|pyverbatim|pycode|pyblock"
    r"|[BL]?[Vv]erbatim\*?)\}"
)
FRAME_BEGIN = re.compile(
    r"\s*\\begin\{frame\}\s*(?:<[^>]*>\s*)?(?P<options>(?:\[[^\]]*\]\s*)*)"
)
FRAGILE_OPTION = re.compile(r"[\[,]\s*fragile\s*[,=\]]")
COMMENT = re.compile(r"\s*%")
@

Inside a block, the rules are those of \textsf{comment}, which reads the lines
until one that's \emph{exactly} [[\end{en}]].
So an indented [[\end{en}]] doesn't end the block, and neither \LaTeX\ nor we
can go on from there: it's an error, reported at that line, which is a good
deal better than the \enquote{File ended while scanning use of [[\next]]} that
\LaTeX\
gives at the end of the file.
For the same reason a chunk that shows [[\end{en}]] in the first column ends
an
\texttt{en} block, whatever noweb thinks, while [[\end{sv}]] means nothing
there.

We record the other errors that a build would otherwise only reveal much
later, or never: an [[\end{en}]] with no block to end; a block that never
ends; text after [[\begin{en}]], which \textsf{comment} silently drops, so
it's neither in the PDF nor in the hash; text after [[\end{en}]], which makes
it no end at all; and a new block inside a block, which is what a forgotten
[[\end{en}]] looks like.
The rule that a frame holding a block must be fragile also belongs here.
It's cheap to check on the source, and in the slides the error from \LaTeX\ is
again the one about the end of the file.
It only looks for the key among a frame's options, in square brackets: a title
may well mention something fragile, and so may the value of a label.
The options may start on a later line, which is why the match sees a few
lines.
Only the environment is checked: in beamer's command form, [[\frame{...}]],
the body is an argument, which no block may be in, and the script doesn't see
that.

A [[Reading]] holds the result: what was found, in order, with an [[OTHER]]
for whatever separates two blocks.
Blank lines and comments leave no trace there, and that's what makes the
pairing rule of the next section so short.
The comments that are stamps are kept apart, with the lines that can hold
[[\ensv]].

<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
<<constants>>=
OTHER = None
LOOK_AHEAD = 5
BYTE_ORDER_MARK = "\ufeff"
<<classes>>=
@dataclass
class Block:
    """A block of one language, its text, and the stamp on its first line."""

    language: str
    first: int
    last: int
    text: str
    stamp: object = None


@dataclass
class Reading:
    """What a source holds, by 0-based line numbers."""

    lines: list
    items: list = field(default_factory=list)
    stamp_lines: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
    text_lines: set = field(default_factory=set)
    errors: list = field(default_factory=list)
    notes: list = field(default_factory=list)
    blank_lines: set = field(default_factory=set)
    comment_lines: set = field(default_factory=set)
    trailing_stamps: dict = field(default_factory=dict)
<<functions>>=
def read_source(text):
    """Read `text` as `LaTeX` will: find the blocks and the other lines."""
    reading = Reading(lines=text.removeprefix(BYTE_ORDER_MARK).split("\n"))
    lines = reading.lines
    number = 0
    frame = None
    while number < len(lines):
        line = lines[number].rstrip("\r")
        <<skip a chunk or a verbatim environment>>
        if not line.strip():
            reading.blank_lines.add(number)
        elif COMMENT.match(line):
            reading.comment_lines.add(number)
            <<note a stamp on a line of its own>>
        elif match := BLOCK_BEGIN.match(line):
            <<read a block>>
        else:
            <<note a line of text>>
        number += 1
    return reading
@

Chunks and verbatim environments are skipped whole, and each counts as
something between two blocks.
A chunk may be followed directly by another, so we look at the first line of
each.
The [[@]] that ends a chunk often starts the documentation on its own line,
[[@ The function ...]], and what follows it there is text like any other.
A verbatim environment may end on the line where it starts, and if it never
ends we had better say so, or everything after it would pass as checked.

<<skip a chunk or a verbatim environment>>=
if CHUNK_START.match(line):
    number = end_of_chunk(lines, number)
    reading.items.append(OTHER)
    if CHUNK_END.match(lines[number]) and lines[number][1:].strip():
        reading.text_lines.add(number)
    number += 1
    continue
if match := VERBATIM_BEGIN.match(line):
    number = end_of_verbatim(lines, number, match.group(1), reading.errors)
    reading.items.append(OTHER)
    number += 1
    continue
<<functions>>=
def end_of_chunk(lines, number):
    """Return the number of the `@` line that ends the chunk at `number`."""
    for index in range(number + 1, len(lines)):
        if CHUNK_END.match(lines[index]):
            return index
    return len(lines) - 1


def end_of_verbatim(lines, number, name, errors):
    """Return the number of the line that ends the environment at `number`."""
    end = "\\end{" + name + "}"
    for index in range(number, len(lines)):
        if end in lines[index]:
            return index
    errors.append((number, f"this environment has no {end}"))
    return len(lines) - 1
@

The lines of text also tell us about frames, and they're where an [[\ensv]] can
be.
An [[\end{en}]] is only stray here: the one that belongs to a block is
consumed when the block is read.

<<note a line of text>>=
reading.items.append(OTHER)
reading.text_lines.add(number)
if match := FRAME_BEGIN.match("\n".join(lines[number : number + LOOK_AHEAD])):
    frame = (number, bool(FRAGILE_OPTION.search(match.group("options"))))
if "\\end{frame}" in line:
    frame = None
elif line in ("\\end{en}", "\\end{sv}"):
    reading.errors.append((number, f"{line} with no block to end"))
@

Reading a block means reading as \textsf{comment} does, and keeping track of
the chunks inside only so that we can say what's wrong if one of them ends the
block.
The text of the block is what's between the two lines: what follows
[[\begin{en}]] on its line is ignored by \textsf{comment}, so we ignore it
too.

<<read a block>>=
language = match.group(1)
if line[match.end() :].strip() and not COMMENT.match(line[match.end() :]):
    reading.errors.append(
        (number, f"text after \\begin{{{language}}} is dropped by LaTeX")
    )
if frame and not frame[1]:
    reading.errors.append(
        (
            number,
            f"this block is in a frame (line {frame[0] + 1}) "
            "that must be [fragile]",
        )
    )
last = end_of_block(lines, number, language, reading.errors)
stamp = STAMP_COMMENT.search(line[match.end() :])
reading.items.append(
    Block(
        language,
        number,
        last,
        without_stamps("\n".join(lines[number + 1 : last])),
        parse_entries(stamp.group("entries"))[0] if stamp else None,
    )
)
number = last
<<constants>>=
INDENTED_END = re.compile(r"\s+\\end\{" + LANGUAGE + r"\}\s*$")
END_WITH_TEXT = re.compile(r"\\end\{" + LANGUAGE + r"\}\s*\S")
<<functions>>=
def end_of_block(lines, number, language, errors):
    """Return the number of the line ending the block at `number`."""
    closer = "\\end{" + language + "}"
    shown = None
    for index in range(number + 1, len(lines)):
        line = lines[index].rstrip(" \r")
        if line == closer:
            if shown:
                errors.append(
                    (index, f"this {closer} in a {shown} ends its block")
                )
            return index
        if shown is None:
            <<check a line of a block>>
        shown = next_shown(line, shown)
    errors.append((number, f"this block has no {closer}"))
    return len(lines) - 1
@

The three checks on a line of a block only make sense for what \LaTeX\ would
read as text if it got that far.
A chunk or a verbatim environment can show all of this.

<<check a line of a block>>=
match = INDENTED_END.match(line)
if match and match.group(1) == language:
    errors.append((index, f"{closer} must be in the first column"))
match = END_WITH_TEXT.match(line)
if match and match.group(1) == language:
    errors.append((index, f"{closer} must be alone on its line"))
if BLOCK_BEGIN.match(line):
    errors.append((index, f"a block inside a block: is {closer} missing?"))
<<functions>>=
def next_shown(line, shown):
    """Return what a line leaves open: a chunk, an environment or `None`."""
    if shown is None:
        if CHUNK_START.match(line):
            return "chunk"
        match = VERBATIM_BEGIN.match(line)
        if match and "\\end{" + match.group(1) + "}" not in line:
            return match.group(1)
        return None
    if shown == "chunk":
        return None if CHUNK_END.match(line) else shown
    return None if "\\end{" + shown + "}" in line else shown
@

Let's verify that the blocks are the ones \LaTeX\ will see, starting with
those that aren't blocks at all.
In the first test the stamp would end up inside the program if the script took
the text for a block.

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_block_in_a_code_chunk_is_not_a_block(repo):
  (repo / "contents.nw").write_text(
    "@<<[[show.tex]]>>=\n" + blocks("en", "sv") + "@\nText.\n"
  )
  result = run(repo, "-v", "contents.nw")
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stdout
  assert "0 pairs" in result.stdout


def test_a_chunk_without_an_end_runs_to_the_end_of_the_file(repo):
