\subsection{Reading a source: what is a string}\label{sec:sync-tr}

An [[\ensv]] is a pair on a smaller scale, with the difference that its parts
aren't on lines of their own: [[\ensv{A \emph{big} cat}{En \emph{stor} katt}]].
Finding one means reading the text lines as \TeX\ will, and that's where
earlier versions of this script went wrong: a [[%]] inside [[\verb|%|]] or a
URL taken for a comment, [[\string\ensv]] taken for a use, a blank line between
the two arguments taken for nothing.
Each of these came from a regular expression that knew one rule of \TeX\ and
not the next, applied in an order that let one pass undo another.

So the text lines are read in one pass, from left to right, into tokens, and
at each place exactly one rule applies.
The line-by-line reading of the previous section stays as it is, since blocks,
chunks and verbatim environments really do end at whole lines, whatever \TeX\
would make of them.
What's left for this pass is the lines that \LaTeX\ reads as text, and between
them the lines that it doesn't: an empty line is a [[\par]], a line that holds
only a comment is nothing at all, and anything else, a chunk or a block, is
something that no argument can run across.

<<constants>>=
OPAQUE = "opaque"
PAR = "par"
<<classes>>=
@dataclass
class Token:
    """A token of `LaTeX` text: its kind, its text and where it is."""

    kind: str
    text: str
    start: int
    end: int
<<functions>>=
def tokens_of(reading):
    """Return the tokens of the text lines of a source, in order."""
    tokens = []
    offset = 0
    for number, line in enumerate(reading.lines):
        if number in reading.text_lines:
            tokens += tokens_of_line(line, offset)
        elif number in reading.blank_lines:
            tokens.append(Token(PAR, "", offset, offset))
        elif number not in reading.comment_lines:
            tokens.append(Token(OPAQUE, "", offset, offset + len(line)))
        offset += len(line) + 1
    return tokens
@

On a text line, a backslash starts a control sequence, a word of letters or a
single other character: [[\\]] is one token, so [[\\ensv]] is a line break and
four letters, and [[\%]] is no comment.
The letters include [[@]], so that [[\ensv@x]], a name in some package's code,
isn't taken for ours.
A [[%]] that's left starts a comment, which runs to the end of the line.
Braces are kept as tokens; everything else is a character that only matters as
something that isn't a brace.

What makes this a reader of \LaTeX\ and not of \TeX\ is a short table of the
commands that read their argument verbatim, where a [[%]] or a brace is a
character like any other.
Each is read whole, as one token: [[\verb]], [[\Verb]] and
[[\SaveVerb]] up to the next delimiter; [[\lstinline]], [[\mintinline]],
[[\mint]] and [[\path]], with a delimiter or in braces; the address of
[[\url]], [[\nolinkurl]] and [[\href]], in which [[%20]] is a space and no
comment; PythonTeX's inline code; and noweb's
quoting of code in double brackets, which isn't \LaTeX\ but is in every
literate source.
A command that isn't in the table is read as \TeX, so a [[%]] in the argument
of a verbatim command of somebody else's still looks like a comment to us.
That's a limit to state, not a rule we could complete: any package may define
such a command, and a document may change what [[%]] means; dbosk/didactic\#106
collects the script's limits.
The harm would be in one direction, a use of ours that we don't see, so we
look for the sign of it: a comment that holds one of our names and starts
inside a group that hasn't closed, as the address of an unknown link command
would.
That gets a note on the standard error, which may be a false alarm about a
comment inside a long [[\ensv]], and leaves the exit status alone.

<<constants>>=
VERBATIM_COMMANDS = {
    "verb", "Verb", "SaveVerb", "lstinline", "mint", "mintinline", "path",
    "url", "nolinkurl", "py", "pyc", "pys", "pyv", "pyb",
}
STARRED = {"verb", "Verb"}
OPTIONS_FIRST = {"Verb", "SaveVerb", "lstinline", "mint", "mintinline", "href"}
GROUP_FIRST = {"SaveVerb", "mint", "mintinline"}
LETTER = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z@]")
<<functions>>=
def tokens_of_line(line, offset):
    """Return the tokens of one line of text, at `offset` in the source."""
    tokens = []
    index = 0
    while index < len(line):
        start = index
        char = line[index]
        if char == "\\":
            index = end_of_name(line, index + 1)
            name = line[start + 1 : index] or " "
            tokens.append(Token("cs", name, offset + start, offset + index))
            if name in VERBATIM_COMMANDS or name == "href":
                verbatim = index
                index = end_of_verbatim_argument(line, index, name)
                tokens.append(
                    Token("verbatim", "", offset + verbatim, offset + index)
                )
        elif line.startswith("[[", index):
            end = line.find("]]", index)
            index = end + 2 if end != -1 else len(line)
            tokens.append(Token("verbatim", "", offset + start, offset + index))
        elif char == "%":
            index = len(line)
            tokens.append(
                Token("comment", line[start:], offset + start, offset + index)
            )
        elif char in "{}":
            index += 1
            tokens.append(Token(char, char, offset + start, offset + index))
        else:
            index += 1
            if not char.isspace():
                token = Token("char", char, offset + start, offset + index)
                tokens.append(token)
    return tokens


def end_of_name(line, index):
    """Return where the name of a control sequence at `index` ends."""
    if index >= len(line):
        return index
    if not LETTER.match(line[index]):
        return index + 1
    while index < len(line) and LETTER.match(line[index]):
        index += 1
    return index
@

The argument of a verbatim command starts after its star, its options in
brackets and, for [[\mintinline]], the language in braces.
Then the first character is the delimiter, unless it's a brace, in which case
the braces count.
Of [[\href]] only the address is verbatim, not the text that follows it.
An argument that doesn't end on its line runs to the end of the line, which is
as far as any of these can reach.

<<functions>>=
def end_of_verbatim_argument(line, index, name):
    """Return where the verbatim argument of command `name` at `index` ends."""
    if name in STARRED and line.startswith("*", index):
        index += 1
    if name != "verb":
        index = skip_spaces(line, index)
    if name in OPTIONS_FIRST and line.startswith("[", index):
        close = line.find("]", index)
        index = skip_spaces(line, close + 1 if close != -1 else len(line))
    if name in GROUP_FIRST and line.startswith("{", index):
        index = skip_spaces(line, end_of_braces(line, index))
    if index >= len(line):
        return index
    if line[index] == "{":
        return end_of_braces(line, index)
    close = line.find(line[index], index + 1)
    return close + 1 if close != -1 else len(line)


def end_of_braces(line, index):
    """Return where the group that opens at `index` ends, or the line's end."""
    depth = 0
    for position in range(index, len(line)):
        if line[position] == "{":
            depth += 1
        elif line[position] == "}":
            depth -= 1
            if depth == 0:
                return position + 1
    return len(line)


def skip_spaces(line, index):
    """Return the first place at or after `index` that isn't a space."""
    while index < len(line) and line[index].isspace():
        index += 1
    return index
