Now the names.
The command is named [[\ensv]], or [[\didactictr]] in a document where
[[\ensv]] is taken.
A [[\tr]] is none of our business: the package defines none, and
\textsf{physics} has one for the trace, so a [[\tr]] in a source is that or
the document's own.

Not every token with our name is a use.
After [[\string]], [[\ifdefined]], [[\noexpand]], [[\show]] or [[\meaning]] it
is only mentioned, and so are the two after [[\ifx]]: a text about this
package, or a preamble that looks before it defines, has those.
After a command that defines one, such as [[\newcommand]], [[\def]] or
[[\NewDocumentCommand]], with or without a star and braces around the name,
it's a definition; and so it is as the first operand of [[\let]], or of
[[\NewCommandCopy]] and its relatives.
The long name is the package's own, which no document has a reason to
redefine, and a file that does can't be checked, so that's an error.
A file that defines [[\ensv]], on the other hand, has its own, so there, as
in the package, the name is the document's: its uses are neither pairs nor
errors, whatever their arguments, and only [[\didactictr]] is read.
The state check says so once, on the standard error, since a deck's pairs
written with [[\ensv]] would otherwise vanish from the report without a word.
What the script can't know is a definition in another file, such as a preamble
that the document inputs, or in a package: there the uses of [[\ensv]] are read
as ours, and [[\didactictr]] is the way out.
Nor can it run \TeX: a definition that \TeX\ never reaches, inside
[[\iffalse]], counts all the same, and so does a [[\providecommand]] after
the package is loaded, which keeps the package's [[\ensv]]; for the latter
the note says that it was a [[\providecommand]].
These, too, are in dbosk/didactic\#106.
Our name as the second operand of [[\let]], [[\let\x\ensv]], is an alias, and
an error, since the strings written with [[\x]] would never be seen.

<<constants>>=
OUR_NAMES = tuple(PAIRS)
LONG_NAME = "didactictr"
SHORT_NAMES = tuple(name for name in OUR_NAMES if name != LONG_NAME)
MENTIONS = {"string": 1, "ifdefined": 1, "noexpand": 1, "show": 1,
            "meaning": 1, "ifx": 2}
DEFINERS = {
    "def", "gdef", "edef", "xdef", "newcommand", "renewcommand",
    "providecommand", "DeclareRobustCommand", "newrobustcmd",
    "renewrobustcmd", "providerobustcmd", "NewDocumentCommand",
    "RenewDocumentCommand", "ProvideDocumentCommand", "DeclareDocumentCommand",
    "NewExpandableDocumentCommand", "RenewExpandableDocumentCommand",
    "ProvideExpandableDocumentCommand", "DeclareExpandableDocumentCommand",
}
COPIERS = {"let", "NewCommandCopy", "RenewCommandCopy", "DeclareCommandCopy"}
OWN_ENSV = "this file defines \\ensv, so it's left alone here; use \\didactictr"
PROVIDED_ENSV = (
    "this file defines \\ensv with \\providecommand, which keeps didactic's "
    "if it comes after it; it's left alone here all the same; use \\didactictr"
)
DOUBTFUL_COMMENT = (
