\subsection{Pairs and stamps}\label{sec:sync-pairs}

Two blocks form a pair when they have different languages and only blank lines
and comments lie between them.
Since a reading holds just the blocks and an [[OTHER]] for everything else,
that's a question of whether they're neighbours in the list; going through it
from the start also settles what three blocks in a row mean: the first two
pair up.
So a block of one edition right before a pair, with only comments between,
pairs with the pair's first block, and the stamp of the pair is then reported
as one with no pair; any line of text between them, [[\relax]] if there's
nothing else to say, keeps them apart.
A block without a partner belongs to one edition only, and that's a normal
thing to want (a pointer to a deck that only one of the courses has), so it
needs no stamp and it's never reported.

A stamp is a comment at the end of a line, and stamping never adds a line or
takes one away, so that the names in a report, which are line numbers, stay
true however many pairs are stamped after it.
A block pair's stamp stands on the line of its first [[\begin]], where
\textsf{comment} ignores whatever follows:
\begin{minted}{text}
\begin{en} % sync en=3f9c1a sv=b20e7d
The cat sat.
\end{en}
\begin{sv}
Katten satt.
\end{sv}
\end{minted}
If the blocks change places, the stamp is still found on the [[\begin]] of the
other; a stamp on both is one too many, and the second is reported.
An [[\ensv]]'s stamp stands on the line where it ends, one entry per use of
[[\ensv]] or [[\didactictr]] that ends there, separated by semicolons, in
the order they appear:
\begin{minted}{text}
\ensv{one}{ett} \ensv{two}{två} % sync en=7692c3 sv=4e07d4; en=ad0234 sv=f4e6a1
\end{minted}
An entry of [[-]] marks an [[\ensv]] on that line that hasn't been stamped yet,
so that the second can be stamped before the first, and they don't change
places.
The uses that a file's own [[\ensv]] leaves alone keep their places in the
count, so that adding such a definition doesn't hand one pair's entry to the
next.

A stamp is never part of the side it stands in.
A pair that starts on the line where another ends, [[\ensv{a}{b} \ensv{c]]
with [[d}{e}]] on the next line, has the first one's stamp inside its own
first argument, and if the stamp counted, every stamp of the first would put
the second out of step.

<<imports>>=
from typing import NamedTuple
<<constants>>=
ENTRY = " ".join(code + r"=(?:[0-9a-f]+|-)" for code in LANGUAGES)
STAMP_COMMENT = re.compile(
    r"\s*%\s*sync\s+(?P<entries>" + ENTRY + r"(?:;\s*" + ENTRY + r")*)\s*$"
)
<<classes>>=
class Stamp(NamedTuple):
    """The hashes of the two sides when they last said the same thing."""

    en: str
    sv: str

    def __str__(self):
        return f"en={self.en} sv={self.sv}"
<<functions>>=
def without_stamps(text):
    """Return `text` without the stamp comments at the ends of its lines."""
    lines = []
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        if match := STAMP_COMMENT.search(line):
            line = line[: match.start()]
        lines.append(line)
    return "\n".join(lines)


def parse_entries(entries):
    """Return the stamps in the text of a stamp comment; `-` gives `None`."""
    stamps = []
    for entry in entries.split(";"):
        en, sv = (part.split("=")[1] for part in entry.split())
        stamps.append(None if "-" in (en, sv) else Stamp(en, sv))
    return stamps
@

A stamp that no pair uses is reported: a block with a stamp and no partner, a
stamp entry on a line where fewer uses end, a stamp at the end of a line where
none ends at all.
It means that a pair has lost a partner, or a use has been rewritten as plain
text.
Saying nothing there would turn a paragraph that had a translation into a
paragraph of one edition only, without anybody having decided that.
If that's the decision, [[--stamp]] at that line removes the stamp
(\cref{sec:sync-stamp}).
A line that is nothing but a stamp comment is how stamps of blocks were
written before they moved to the [[\begin]] line; no pair has one now, so it
is reported in the same way.

