Here is the hash.
It's of the whole text of a side, a comment in it included, with all white
space in the prose normalised, because re-wrapping a paragraph doesn't change
what it says, and a report after each re-wrap would teach everybody to stamp
without reading.
That doesn't hold for code: moving a line of a chunk in or out of a loop
changes the program, and so does joining two lines of a script.
So the lines of a chunk or a verbatim environment in a block stay whole, with
their indentation.
Six hexadecimal digits are plenty: a hash only has to differ from the previous
hash of the same paragraph.
A change that happens to keep it goes unseen, but with 24 bits that's about
one change in 17 million.

<<imports>>=
import hashlib
<<constants>>=
HASH_LENGTH = 6
<<functions>>=
def side_hash(text):
    """Return the short hash of one side, ignoring how its prose is spaced."""
    normal = normal_form(text).encode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")
    return hashlib.sha256(normal).hexdigest()[:HASH_LENGTH]


def normal_form(text):
    """Return a side with its prose as words and its code as whole lines."""
    parts = []
    shown = None
    for line in text.split("\n"):
        after = next_shown(line, shown)
        if shown or after:
            parts.append(line.rstrip())
        else:
            parts += line.split()
        shown = after
    return "\n".join(parts)
@

A [[Pair]] then has all that the modes need, whichever way it came about.
The [[target]] is how the report names it: the line, followed by which [[\ensv]]
on the line if there are several.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass
class Pair:
    """Two sides that should say the same thing, and the stamp above them."""

    target: str
    first: int
    last: int
    en: str
    sv: str
    stamp: Stamp | None
    is_block: bool = False
    other_begin: int = 0
    position: int = 0
    uses_on_line: int = 1

    def current(self):
        """Return the stamp the two sides would get now."""
        return Stamp(side_hash(self.en), side_hash(self.sv))
<<functions>>=
def block_pairs(reading):
    """Return the pairs of blocks in a source, and the stamps they don't use."""
    pairs = []
    unused = []
    items = reading.items
    index = 0
    while index < len(items):
        one = items[index]
        two = items[index + 1] if index + 1 < len(items) else OTHER
        if one is OTHER:
            index += 1
            continue
        if two is OTHER or one.language == two.language:
            if one.stamp:
                unused.append(one.first)
            index += 1
            continue
        if one.stamp and two.stamp:
            unused.append(two.first)
        sides = {one.language: one.text, two.language: two.text}
        pairs.append(
            Pair(
                str(one.first + 1),
                one.first,
                two.last,
                sides["en"],
                sides["sv"],
                one.stamp or two.stamp,
                is_block=True,
                other_begin=two.first,
            )
        )
        index += 2
    return pairs, unused
@

The pairs of [[\ensv]] are simpler, since nothing can separate their sides.
Their stamps are grouped by the line where they end, so that the entries on a
line go with the [[\ensv]]s in order, however many there are.

<<functions>>=
def ensv_pairs(reading):
    """Return the pairs that the uses of `\\ensv` make, and unused stamps."""
    by_line = {}
    for use in find_ensv(reading):
        by_line.setdefault(use.last, []).append(use)
    pairs = []
    unused = []
    for line in sorted(set(by_line) | set(reading.trailing_stamps)):
        uses = by_line.get(line, [])
        entries = reading.trailing_stamps.get(line)
        stamps = parse_entries(entries) if entries else []
        if len(stamps) > len(uses):
            unused.append(line)
        for position, use in enumerate(uses):
            if use.own:
                continue
            target = str(line + 1)
            if len(uses) > 1:
                target += f".{position + 1}"
            stamp = stamps[position] if position < len(stamps) else None
            pairs.append(
                Pair(
                    target,
                    use.first,
                    line,
                    use.en,
                    use.sv,
                    stamp,
                    position=position,
                    uses_on_line=len(uses),
                )
            )
    return pairs, unused
@

Now it comes together, for a text from wherever it comes, with the name of
its file, which says whether noweb's quoting is quoting there.
The stamp lines that no pair used are returned too, and the notes for the
report.

<<functions>>=
def read_pairs(text, name=""):
    """Return a source's pairs, in order, unused stamp lines, errors, notes."""
    reading = read_source(text)
    pairs, unused_by_blocks = block_pairs(reading)
    more_pairs, unused_by_ensv = ensv_pairs(reading)
    pairs = sorted(pairs + more_pairs, key=lambda pair: pair.first)
    leftover = sorted(
        set(reading.stamp_lines) | set(unused_by_blocks) | set(unused_by_ensv)
    )
    return pairs, leftover, reading.errors, reading.notes
