\subsection{The word diff, and the commit of the stamp}\label{sec:sync-diff}

A report that a side is behind isn't yet much help.
What whoever translates needs to see is what changed in the one side, next to
what the other side says now.
In a paragraph that was re-wrapped on the way, a line diff would show the
whole paragraph as changed, so it's a diff of words, printed in the notation
of [[git diff --word-diff]].
The words are those that the hash compares, from [[normal_form]]: in the prose
a word, but in a chunk or a verbatim environment a whole line with its
indentation.
Splitting the code into words as well would make a re-indented or a joined
line look the same before and after, and the report would say that a side is
behind while showing no change in it.

<<imports>>=
import difflib
<<functions>>=
def word_diff(old, new):
    """Return a diff of two texts by words, as in `git diff --word-diff`."""
    before = normal_form(old).split("\n")
    after = normal_form(new).split("\n")
    matcher = difflib.SequenceMatcher(None, before, after, autojunk=False)
    parts = []
    for tag, i1, i2, j1, j2 in matcher.get_opcodes():
        if tag == "equal":
            parts += before[i1:i2]
            continue
        change = ""
        if i2 > i1:
            change += "[-" + " ".join(before[i1:i2]) + "-]"
        if j2 > j1:
            change += "{+" + " ".join(after[j1:j2]) + "+}"
        parts.append(change)
    return " ".join(parts)
@

To diff against the text as it was stamped we need that text, and the stamp is
how to find it: the commit that wrote this stamp is where the two sides said
the same thing.
[[git log -S]] gives the commits that changed the number of occurrences of a
string, which for a stamp means those that wrote or removed it, and with
[[--name-only]] it says in which files.
We don't limit it to the file at hand, since the pair may have been moved to
another one since.
File names come back separated by NUL with [[-z]], since git would otherwise
quote the ones with unusual characters, a Swedish letter being one, and we
would look for a file by its quoted name; a mark that no file name starts with
tells the commits from the names.

A file in such a commit is read with the same [[read_pairs]] as the file on
disk, to find the pair with this stamp and check that the side has the stamped
hash.
That check also rules out a commit that removed the stamp, and a stamp that
was written by hand.
If nothing is found, the stamp stands outside of history: it isn't committed
yet, or it has been made up, or this isn't a git repository at all.
It's still as stale as it was, so the report is given anyway without the diff.

After a large edit, a book has many pairs behind in the same few files, and
the commits that wrote their stamps are mostly the same few too.
Reading such a file at such a commit again for every pair made the report take
minutes on a large file, so what a file holds at a commit is read once and
kept, and so is the top of the repository.
Both are only ever read, never changed, within a run.
The variable [[DIDACTIC_SYNC_NO_CACHE]] turns the keeping off, which is how
the tests show that it changes nothing in the report.

<<imports>>=
import subprocess
import functools
import os
<<constants>>=
COMMIT_MARK = "%x01"
<<functions>>=
def git(directory, *args):
    """Return the output of a git command, or `None` if it fails."""
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["git", *args],
            cwd=directory,
            capture_output=True,
            check=True,
        )
    except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, FileNotFoundError):
        return None
    return done.stdout.decode("utf-8", "surrogateescape")


def text_at_stamp(path, pair):
    """Return the commit that wrote a pair's stamp, and the pair there."""
    root = top_of(Path(path).resolve().parent)
    if root is None:
        return None
    log = git(
        root,
        "log",
        f"-S{pair.stamp}",
        f"--format={COMMIT_MARK}%h",
        "--name-only",
        "-z",
        "HEAD",
    )
    if log is None:
        return None
    commit = None
    for entry in log.split("\0"):
        name = entry.lstrip("\n")
        if name.startswith("\x01"):
            commit = name[1:]
        elif name:
            pairs = pairs_in_commit(root, commit, name)
            found = stamped_pair(pairs, pair.stamp)
            if found:
                return commit, found
    return None


def stamped_pair(pairs, stamp):
    """Return the pair whose sides have the hashes of `stamp`."""
    for pair in pairs:
        if pair.stamp == stamp and pair.current() == stamp:
            return pair
    return None


def kept(function):
    """Return `function` with its results kept, unless that's turned off."""
    if os.environ.get("DIDACTIC_SYNC_NO_CACHE"):
        return function
    return functools.cache(function)


@kept
def top_of(directory):
    """Return the top of the repository that holds `directory`, or `None`."""
    root = git(directory, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    return root.strip() if root is not None else None


@kept
def pairs_in_commit(root, commit, name):
    """Return the pairs of a file as it is at a commit, read once."""
    text = git(root, "show", f"{commit}:{name}")
    return read_pairs(text, name)[0] if text else []
@

The report then gives the diff of each side that has changed, and the current
text of a side that hasn't.
Without a commit, the current texts of both sides are all that we have, and
they're still what the reader needs to go on.

<<functions>>=
def print_sides(path, pair, state):
    """Print the two sides of a pair that isn't in sync."""
    found = text_at_stamp(path, pair)
    sides = (
        ("en", pair.en, state != EN_BEHIND),
        ("sv", pair.sv, state != SV_BEHIND),
    )
    if found is None:
        print("  (no commit found for this stamp, so no diff)")
    for language, text, changed in sides:
        if changed and found is not None:
            commit, old = found
            change = word_diff(getattr(old, language), text)
            print(f"  {language} since the stamp ({commit}): {change}")
        else:
            print(f"  {language} now: {' '.join(text.split())}")
