@

\subsection{Stamping one pair}\label{sec:sync-stamp}

A stamp is a judgement about one pair, so the command takes one pair, by the
name that the report gives it: the line of a block pair's first [[\begin]], or
the line where an [[\ensv]] ends, followed by which one if several end there,
[[contents.nw:12.2]].
Any other line of the pair is refused, with the pair's name, instead of taken
to mean the pair it lies in.
A line can lie in several pairs, as an [[\ensv]] over several lines shares its
first line with whatever ends there, and only the name is certain to mean one.
Since stamping never changes the number of lines, the names in a report stay
right however many of its pairs are stamped one after another; it's an edit
by hand that moves them, and after one the report is run again anyway.
The command says what it stamped, with the start of both sides, so that a slip
of the finger shows.
There's no form for a whole file and no way to give several targets, which is
deliberate: see the start of this section.

<<constants>>=
TARGET = re.compile(r"(?P<path>.+):(?P<line>[0-9]+)(?:\.(?P<which>[0-9]+))?$")
SHOWN_WORDS = 6
<<functions>>=
def find_target(pairs, line, which):
    """Return the pair with a report's name, or a message why there's none."""
    named = f"{line}.{which}" if which is not None else str(line)
    for pair in pairs:
        if pair.target == named:
            return pair, None
    around = [p.target for p in pairs if p.first <= line - 1 <= p.last]
    if around:
        names = ", ".join(around)
        return None, f"no pair is named {named}; the line is in {names}"
    return None, f"no pair is named {named}"


def shown(side):
    """Return the start of a side, for saying what was stamped."""
    words = side.split()
    more = " ..." if len(words) > SHOWN_WORDS else ""
    return " ".join(words[:SHOWN_WORDS]) + more
@

Writing the stamp changes one line and nothing else, which is why the text is
handled as a list of the lines as they are in the file.
A line ending in a carriage return keeps it, so that a CRLF file doesn't get
mixed line ends, and a byte order mark stays the first thing in the file; the
reader doesn't see it.
A side with no text has nothing to agree with, so a pair isn't stamped until
both sides are written, and a comment such as [[% TODO translate]] doesn't
count as written.
A word that one language has and the other hasn't, such as an article, is
written [[\ensv{the }{\relax}]], which counts as text and prints nothing.
A stamp with no pair is removed the same way, from the end of its line; only a
line that is nothing but a stamp, as they were written before, goes as a whole,
and the command says that the lines after it have moved up.
On a line where uses of [[\ensv]] end, only the entries beyond them go: the
others still belong to their pairs, and taking them too would turn a pair that
is behind into a new one, its change no longer shown.

<<functions>>=
def has_text(side):
    """Tell whether a side holds anything but blank lines and comments."""
    return any(
        line.strip() and not COMMENT.match(line) for line in side.split("\n")
    )


def stamp_pair(target):
    """Write a pair's current stamp to a file; return an error message."""
    match = TARGET.match(target)
    if match is None:
        return "name a pair as FILE:LINE or FILE:LINE.N"
    path = match.group("path")
    line = int(match.group("line"))
    if not Path(path).is_file():
        return f"{path}: no such file"
    text = read_text(path)
    mark = BYTE_ORDER_MARK if text.startswith(BYTE_ORDER_MARK) else ""
    pairs, leftover, errors, _ = read_pairs(text, path)
    if errors:
        return f"{path}:{errors[0][0] + 1}: {errors[0][1]}"
    lines = text.removeprefix(mark).split("\n")
    pair, message = find_target(pairs, line, match.group("which"))
    if pair is None and line - 1 in leftover and match.group("which") is None:
        here = [p for p in pairs if not p.is_block and p.last == line - 1]
        uses = max((p.uses_on_line for p in here), default=0)
        print(remove_stamp(lines, line - 1, path, uses))
        write_text(path, mark + "\n".join(lines))
        return None
    if pair is None:
        return f"{path}: {message}"
    if not has_text(pair.en) or not has_text(pair.sv):
        return f"{path}:{line}: stamp once both sides are written"
    if pair.is_block:
        write_block_stamp(lines, pair)
    else:
        write_tr_stamp(lines, pair)
    write_text(path, mark + "\n".join(lines))
    sides = f"en {shown(pair.en)} / sv {shown(pair.sv)}"
    print(f"stamped {path}:{pair.target}: {sides}")
    <<warn if the branch check can't see [[path]]>>
    return None
@

A stamp is put at the end of a line, after the text and a space, or replaces
the one that's there; taking it out leaves the text as it was.
A block pair's stamp goes on its first [[\begin]] line, and a stamp on the
other one, from before the blocks changed places, goes.

<<functions>>=
def with_stamp(line, entries):
    """Return a line with its stamp comment replaced, or taken out if `None`."""
    ending = "\r" if line.endswith("\r") else ""
    body = line.removesuffix("\r")
    if match := STAMP_COMMENT.search(body):
        body = body[: match.start()].rstrip()
    if entries is None:
        return body + ending
    return f"{body.rstrip()} % sync {entries}{ending}"


def write_block_stamp(lines, pair):
    """Put the stamp of a block pair on its first begin line."""
    lines[pair.first] = with_stamp(lines[pair.first], str(pair.current()))
    other = lines[pair.other_begin]
    if STAMP_COMMENT.search(other.removesuffix("\r")):
        lines[pair.other_begin] = with_stamp(other, None)


def remove_stamp(lines, number, path, uses):
    """Take out a stamp, or the entries of one, that no pair uses."""
    if COMMENT.match(lines[number]) and STAMP_COMMENT.fullmatch(
        lines[number].removesuffix("\r")
    ):
        del lines[number]
        where = f"{path}:{number + 1}"
        return f"removed the line {where}; the lines after it moved up"
    if uses:
        match = STAMP_COMMENT.search(lines[number].removesuffix("\r"))
        entries = match.group("entries").split(";")[:uses]
        kept = "; ".join(entry.strip() for entry in entries)
        lines[number] = with_stamp(lines[number], kept)
        return f"removed the entries with no pair at {path}:{number + 1}"
    lines[number] = with_stamp(lines[number], None)
    return f"removed the stamp at {path}:{number + 1}"
@

A stamp of an [[\ensv]] rewrites the entries of its line: the new entry in its
place, the others as they were, or as [[-]] where there was none, and as many
as there are uses on the line, so that a surplus left by a rewritten use goes.

<<functions>>=
def write_tr_stamp(lines, pair):
    """Replace or append the stamp comment where an `\\ensv` ends."""
    line = lines[pair.last]
    match = STAMP_COMMENT.search(line.removesuffix("\r"))
    old = parse_entries(match.group("entries")) if match else []
    entries = []
    for position in range(pair.uses_on_line):
        stamp = old[position] if position < len(old) else None
        if position == pair.position:
            stamp = pair.current()
        entries.append(str(stamp) if stamp else "en=- sv=-")
    lines[pair.last] = with_stamp(line, "; ".join(entries))
@

Let's verify the promise about the other lines on a file with a bit of
everything, including a last line without a line feed; the bytes of each line
are compared.

