The same string often occurs several times in a deck, with the same stamp, so
these have to be counted and can't just be compared as sets: one of two equal
pairs may be re-stamped while its twin keeps the old stamp.
A refreshed pair is matched with a stamp that went away in the same file
first, in order, as a pair rarely moves to another file while it's being
fixed.

Stamps alone can't always tell what happened, and the listing errs on the side
of showing too much.
If a pair is changed and re-stamped and the branch also adds a new pair equal
to what the first one was, then the old stamp is still there and no stamp went
away: this looks exactly like a new pair beside an untouched one.
So a new stamp that shares one hash with a stamp that's still there is listed
too, under a label of its own, \enquote{new, or re-stamped}.
Likewise, if both sides of a pair are rewritten and one of the new sides
happens to equal a side of another pair that also went away, the two look like
one refresh, and the listing shows the wrong old text.
A pair listed for no reason costs a look at it in the review, which is the
cheap direction to be wrong in.
Pairs that exchange sides among themselves, two or three of them, are listed,
for instance: each new stamp shares one hash with a stamp that went away.

What escapes the listing is what the stamps can't show, and what the check
doesn't read; these limits, with the wrong old text above, are collected in
dbosk/didactic\#106.
\begin{description}
  \item[A stamp that was there already] isn't new, so a pair that a branch
  makes equal to another stamped pair, and re-stamps, isn't listed if that
  other pair goes away in the same range: [[\ensv{Yes}{Ja}]] and
  [[\ensv{Yeah}{Ja}]], both stamped, of which only [[\ensv{Yeah}{Ja}]] is
  left.
  The same goes for two pairs that exchange a side when their other sides are
  equal, [[\ensv{Yes}{Ja}]] and [[\ensv{Yeah}{Ja}]] becoming each other: the
  file then holds the same pairs as before in another order.
  Nothing is lost by either: the two sides of each pair were stamped as saying
  the same thing before.
  \item[A change to both sides] isn't a one-side re-stamp, however small the
  second change is: a fix in one language with a comment edited in the other is
  stamped like a translation and isn't listed.
  The check trusts that whoever changed both sides read both; it shows where
  one side was accepted as it stood, not whether anybody read with care.
  \item[Other files] aren't read: the check reads only sources, the files
  whose names end in [[.nw]] or [[.tex]], although the state check and
  [[--stamp]] work on any file; [[--stamp]] warns when it stamps another one.
  \item[A file that can't be read at the start] hides the pairs after the place
  where reading fails, so a re-stamp of one of them isn't listed; see below.
  \item[A file's own [[\ensv]] at the start] makes its uses of [[\ensv]] no
  pairs there, so a branch that takes the definition out and re-stamps such a
  use with one side changed isn't listed.
  \item[A change that keeps the hash] goes unseen by the state check as well;
  the chance is about one in 17 million for each change
  (\cref{sec:sync-pairs}).
\end{description}

The range means what it means to [[git diff]], whose two forms it takes, and
what we need of it is the two commits.
[[A...B]] is what [[B]] did since it left [[A]], from their merge base: that's
the one for a pull request.
[[A..B]] compares the two ends as they are, so a pair that [[A]] re-stamped
after [[B]] left shows up too, the other way round; unlike [[git log A..B]],
it isn't only the commits of [[B]].
All the files the range changed are read at both ends, deleted ones too, so a
pair that has moved between files is still matched.
The suffixes keep us out of files that aren't sources: a program or a data
file may well hold [[\begin{en}]] or [[\ensv]] as text, and reading it as blocks
would give errors, which would fail the check.
A stamp that [[--stamp]] writes into such a file is one that the check won't
list, so it says so.

