\subsection{The command line}\label{sec:sync-cli}

What's left is to tell the three modes apart and to turn the outcome into an
exit status.
A range looks like a range and isn't a file; an argument with two dots that
happens to be an existing file is the file, and so is one named like a source.
What a report prints is the text of the source, which may hold bytes that
aren't UTF-8, so the two streams let them through as they are.
Mixing a range with files has no meaning, and neither has a target for
[[--stamp]] and anything else, so those are errors rather than something
that's silently ignored.
A deliberate side of that: giving [[--stamp]] a second target is an error
instead of a convenience, and [[allow_abbrev]] is off so that nothing else can
be taken for a shorthand of it.

The exit status is an interface: build scripts read 1 as \enquote{something is
behind}.
Python's own way of failing, a traceback and status 1, would read as just
that, so a file that can't be read or written is an error of ours, with
status~2 and one line saying why, and so is anything we didn't foresee, with
the traceback for whoever has to fix it.

<<imports>>=
import argparse
import traceback
<<constants>>=
STATUS_OK = 0
STATUS_NOT_IN_SYNC = 1
STATUS_ERROR = 2
<<functions>>=
def is_range(argument):
    """Tell a revision range from a file name."""
    return (
        ".." in argument
        and not Path(argument).exists()
        and not argument.endswith(STAMPED_SUFFIXES)
    )


def parse_arguments(argv):
    """Return the command line, parsed."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="didactic-sync.py",
        description=__doc__.splitlines()[0],
        allow_abbrev=False,
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "targets",
        nargs="*",
        metavar="FILE|RANGE",
        help="the files to check, or a range to list the pairs it re-stamped "
        "with only one side changed: A...B (as for a pull request) is what B "
        "did since it left A; A..B compares the two ends, as git diff does",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--stamp",
        action="append",
        metavar="FILE:LINE[.N]",
        help="stamp the one pair at that line, after reading both sides",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--list",
        action="store_true",
        help="only list: exit with 0 even if a pair is not in sync",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-v",
        "--verbose",
        action="store_true",
        help="list the pairs that are in sync too",
    )
    return parser.parse_args(argv)


def main(argv=None):
    """Run the mode that the command line asks for; return the exit status."""
    args = parse_arguments(argv)
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(errors="surrogateescape")
    sys.stderr.reconfigure(errors="surrogateescape")
    try:
        return run_mode(args)
    except OSError as error:
        print(f"{error.filename}: {error.strerror}", file=sys.stderr)
    except Exception:
        traceback.print_exc()
        print("internal error", file=sys.stderr)
    return STATUS_ERROR


def run_mode(args):
    """Run the mode that the arguments ask for; return the exit status."""
    if args.stamp is not None:
        if args.targets or len(args.stamp) > 1:
            print("--stamp takes one pair and nothing else", file=sys.stderr)
            return STATUS_ERROR
        message = stamp_pair(args.stamp[0])
        if message:
            print(message, file=sys.stderr)
            return STATUS_ERROR
        return STATUS_OK
    ranges = [target for target in args.targets if is_range(target)]
    if ranges:
        if len(args.targets) > 1:
            print("a range takes no other arguments", file=sys.stderr)
            return STATUS_ERROR
        return check_branch(ranges[0])
    if not args.targets:
        print("name the files to check", file=sys.stderr)
        return STATUS_ERROR
    return check_files(args.targets, args.verbose, args.list)
@

The files are checked independently, and a missing or broken one doesn't stop
the others from being reported.
The status is the worst outcome over all of them, and [[--list]] takes away
only the one that says that something isn't in sync: a build that lists is
still told if the file can't be read as blocks, since it isn't going to build
anyway.

<<functions>>=
def check_files(paths, verbose, list_only):
    """Report on the files; return the exit status."""
    not_in_sync = 0
    broken = False
    for path in paths:
        if not Path(path).is_file():
            print(f"{path}: no such file", file=sys.stderr)
            broken = True
            continue
        try:
            count, errors = check_file(path, verbose)
        except OSError as error:
            print(f"{path}: {error.strerror}", file=sys.stderr)
            broken = True
            continue
        not_in_sync += count
        broken = broken or errors
    if broken:
        return STATUS_ERROR
    if not_in_sync and not list_only:
        return STATUS_NOT_IN_SYNC
    return STATUS_OK
